ale ref under `gloss/` is locked. Verified.** `clearBlockingTracking()` runs before the `try` in `fetchTracking()` (`src/commands/sync.ts`, ~line 76–90). If `update-ref --stdin` cannot lock a stale child ref, the raw error escapes with no `git remote prune` hint, even though that is the exact case the hint exists for. I reproduced it: an empty `refs/notes/remotes/origin/gloss/x.lock` next to a stale `refs/notes/remotes/origin/gloss/x` makes `git-gloss sync` exit 1 with `fatal: cannot lock ref 'refs/notes/remotes/origin/gloss/x' … File exists`, and no hint. The test \"a fetch that still fails says what to run\" only covers a lock on the fetch target, so it misses this path. Fix: move `clearBlockingTracking` inside the `try`, or wrap it the same way, and add a test for a lock on a child ref.\n\n2. **`config-unverifiable` — a git failure before the base loop escapes as a non-ConfigError. Read.** In `trustedRef()` (`src/config.ts` ~line 189), the `rev-parse --verify -q HEAD` call goes through `gitAnswer` outside the `try`. Only exit 1 is treated as \"no answer\". Any other failure throws a plain `GitError`. `withSignals` (`src/floor.ts:208`) rethrows anything that is not a `ConfigError`, so `show`, `lint`, `html` and `hunks` would fail instead of going without signals, which is what the README and the config comment say. I could not produce an exit-128 HEAD in a scratch repo, so this is from reading only. Fix: check HEAD inside the same try, or treat a non-1 failure as `failed`.\n\n3. **Minor doc wording. Read.** The config comment says `show` and `hunks` \"go without signals\", but `html` (`src/html/data.ts:227`) also goes through `tryAnalyze` and is not mentioned. This is accurate in behaviour, only incomplete in the text.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n4. **`config-unverifiable` — no caching of the error. Read.** `loadConfig()` caches only successful results, so a `ConfigError` is recomputed on every call. Per-commit callers such as `show` and `html` re-run `merge-base`, `ls-tree` and `show` for each commit. The warning is still printed only once, so this costs time, not correctness.\n\n5. **`sync-clear` — the namespace overlap is not stated. Read.** A remote named `a/gloss` has tracking refs under `refs/notes/remotes/a/gloss/…`, which sit under the prefix `sync` clears for remote `a`. This is a real D/F conflict, so deleting those refs is required to fetch `a`. It does mean the two remotes evict each other's tracking refs on every sync. This is niche, and it is not in the author's risk list.\n\n**Confirmed as annotated**\n\n- `sync-clear`: the deletion is limited to `refs/notes/remotes/<remote>/gloss/` (and `gloss-reviews/` for the reviews tracking ref). Anything under the fetched tracking ref blocks that fetch, so the README's \"that would block the fetch\" is accurate. Nothing reads those children, which supports the \"assumed always stale\" risk. **Verified:** `test/syncref.test.ts` passes 4/4 (the 3000-ref case ran in 531 ms in this run).\n- `config-unverifiable`: `add` and `signals` call `loadConfig` directly and error out, `lint` reports `incomplete`, and `show` warns and continues. **Verified:** `test/signals.test.ts` passes 47/47, including the unverifiable-config test.\n- TODO's \"opposite move is not cleared\": `fetchTracking` only runs when `ls-ref` finds `refs/notes/gloss` on the remote, so a remote that only has `gloss/x` is not cleared. **Read.**\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
